 (Accenture)) If a business context is provided, tailor your analysis to consider:\n   - The business\'s current position in its industry\n   - Its core competencies and capabilities\n   - Its strategic direction and goals\n   - Its potential vulnerabilities to these market forces\n   - the business is Accenture\n\n**First-Order Implications Criteria:** To qualify as a valid first-order implication, each must:\n   1. Have a direct logical connection to one or more market forces\n   2. Represent a meaningful change or shift requiring strategic attention\n   3. Have observable manifestations or measurable impacts\n   4. Be relevant to the Global Market Forces affecting Management Consulting domain and Accenture context if provided\n   5. Have clear temporal dimensions (near, mid, or long-term)\n\n**Categorisation Framework:** Categorise each implication according to:\n   - Type (Opportunity, Threat, Mixed)\n   - Time Horizon (1-2 years, 3-5 years, 5+ years)\n   - Impact Level (Low, Medium, High)\n   - Uncertainty Level (Low, Medium, High)\n   - Primary Domain (Economic, Social, Technological, Environmental, Political, Legal)\n\n**Output Structure:** For each first-order implication identified, include:\n   1. A clear, concise title\n   2. A detailed description with reasoning\n   3. The parent market force(s) \n    - Make sure you keep the wording of this exactly as it is in the market forces output \n    - The details of the parent market force(s) are the raw_market_force_id & raw_market_force_name from the market forces output.\n    This ensures that it is clear which market force(s) an implication is derived from\n   4. Categorisation according to the framework\n   5. Business relevance if applicable\n   6. Strategic considerations\n\n**Quality Control:** Ensure each implication:\n   - Is truly first-order (direct from market forces, not derived from other implications)\n   - Is substantive (not trivial or obvious)\n   - Is well-reasoned with clear causality\n   - 